WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.321 --> 00:00:03.279
- And you'd have a very hard
time finding anyone cooler

00:00:03.279 --> 00:00:05.612
than the two people we
have on the line right now.

00:00:05.612 --> 00:00:07.321
I can't believe they're in
my show. I'm very excited.

00:00:07.321 --> 00:00:08.286
We are obsessed with them.

00:00:08.286 --> 00:00:09.172
Everyone is,

00:00:09.172 --> 00:00:11.358
and one of them even wrote
the song I just sang,

00:00:11.358 --> 00:00:13.222
which is why I was actually
even more terrified

00:00:13.222 --> 00:00:14.984
to be singing Jolene (chuckles).

00:00:14.984 --> 00:00:15.817
(audience laughing)

00:00:15.817 --> 00:00:17.811
She sold over a hundred million albums.

00:00:17.811 --> 00:00:20.048
He sold over 300 million books.

00:00:20.048 --> 00:00:23.224
So together they have a new
novel called "Run Roses Run."

00:00:23.224 --> 00:00:24.121
It's out now,

00:00:24.121 --> 00:00:26.485
but more importantly they're good people

00:00:26.485 --> 00:00:28.209
and support good courses like literacy,

00:00:28.209 --> 00:00:29.596
health and community.

00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:33.227
Let's say hello to miss the Dolly Parton

00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:34.786
and James Paterson.

00:00:34.786 --> 00:00:39.143
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:39.143 --> 00:00:39.976
- Hey!

00:00:39.976 --> 00:00:42.374
- Oh my gosh it's so
great to see both of you.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:45.475
Thank you so much for taking
the time to do my show.

00:00:45.475 --> 00:00:48.037
- Well we love your show and we love you.

00:00:48.037 --> 00:00:51.076
Thank you for having me.
- Oh! thank you.

00:00:51.076 --> 00:00:53.136
Well, I have to say again,

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:55.672
it was great to sing your music last week

00:00:55.672 --> 00:00:56.981
at the Academy of Country Music Awards,

00:00:56.981 --> 00:00:59.388
but I will say it's always, always,

00:00:59.388 --> 00:01:01.856
always going to be
terrifying to sing your song

00:01:01.856 --> 00:01:03.371
in front of you while you're there.

00:01:03.371 --> 00:01:05.974
Just so you know, I'm
always sweating profusely.

00:01:05.974 --> 00:01:07.229
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:09.443
- Oh well I mean, I don't
think you have to worry

00:01:09.443 --> 00:01:10.631
with a voice like yours.

00:01:10.631 --> 00:01:12.368
I was so honored and
nobody can sing like you.

00:01:12.368 --> 00:01:13.474
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:01:13.474 --> 00:01:16.467
So I was very proud of you and so proud

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.560
of your rendition of it.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.393
It's great.

00:01:18.393 --> 00:01:19.976
One of the best ever, ever, ever.

00:01:19.976 --> 00:01:20.809
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:01:20.809 --> 00:01:22.813
- Oh, you are so kind.

00:01:22.813 --> 00:01:23.817
And it was also,

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:25.407
we haven't really been
able to talk about it,

00:01:25.407 --> 00:01:26.626
but it was cool to sing 9 to 5.

00:01:26.626 --> 00:01:28.793
We did the kind of reimagined version

00:01:28.793 --> 00:01:30.182
of 9 to 5 for your documentary

00:01:30.182 --> 00:01:33.020
Still Working 9 to 5 which is amazing.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.393
Not just the song but the
documentary is amazing.

00:01:35.393 --> 00:01:36.897
So thank you for letting
me be a part of that.

00:01:36.897 --> 00:01:38.897
I got to brag to Blake Shelton

00:01:38.897 --> 00:01:39.805
about that moment.
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:39.805 --> 00:01:41.600
(audience laughs)

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.143
- Well you did a great job on that too.

00:01:44.143 --> 00:01:46.355
I love how you took that
and just made it your own.

00:01:46.355 --> 00:01:49.615
And I got a chance to sing
background for a change.

00:01:49.615 --> 00:01:51.736
But I'm really proud of
that rendition of it.

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:54.106
And I think hopefully
it'll do well for us.

00:01:54.106 --> 00:01:55.441
It's great in the documentary.

00:01:55.441 --> 00:01:56.274
(audience clapping)

00:01:56.274 --> 00:01:57.107
- Oh my God.

00:01:57.107 --> 00:01:57.969
It's a really cool thing.

00:01:57.969 --> 00:02:01.875
James do you have a favorite Dolly song?

00:02:01.875 --> 00:02:04.373
- Yeah well, first of
all also the new album,

00:02:04.373 --> 00:02:05.206
Run Rose Run,

00:02:05.206 --> 00:02:07.436
I love Big Dreams and Faded Jeans

00:02:07.436 --> 00:02:10.650
and Woman Up (And Take
It Like A Man), right.

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.833
- I love that one.
- Right Dolly?

00:02:11.833 --> 00:02:12.666
- I love (chuckles)

00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:13.694
- Yeah, he's always
loved that one (chuckles)

00:02:13.694 --> 00:02:17.501
- And there's My Life
Would Suck Without You.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:18.397
I love that one.

00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:19.230
- Yes!

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.195
- Oh, wait a minute, it's yours Kelly.

00:02:20.195 --> 00:02:21.107
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:25.368
- Yes, James Patterson
knows my music (chuckles)

00:02:25.368 --> 00:02:27.036
Oh my God, that's amazing.

00:02:27.036 --> 00:02:28.500
Did y'all know each other?

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.872
How did you end up working
together on this book?

00:02:30.872 --> 00:02:31.705
- Dolly.

00:02:31.705 --> 00:02:33.647
- You won't tell it? Well-
- I came down to Nashville.

00:02:33.647 --> 00:02:34.480
I went banging,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:35.655
I came down to Nashville.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:38.267
I went to college in
Nashville at Vanderbilt.

00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:39.654
So I loved that town anyway.

00:02:39.654 --> 00:02:42.748
And I used to go to the Grand
Ole Opry when it was in town.

00:02:42.748 --> 00:02:44.506
And I just went banging
on her door and I said,

00:02:44.506 --> 00:02:46.071
you wanna write a book?

00:02:46.071 --> 00:02:46.904
(Kelly chuckles)

00:02:46.904 --> 00:02:47.737
She said, who are you?

00:02:47.737 --> 00:02:48.570
- And,

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:49.903
(all laughing)

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:53.143
- Hardly, actually, I was very honored

00:02:53.143 --> 00:02:54.359
that he would ask me to write a book.

00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:57.292
When he first asked if I'd
like to write a book with him.

00:02:57.292 --> 00:02:58.963
And I thought, well,
what do you need me for?

00:02:58.963 --> 00:03:01.234
You only sold 400 million books so far.

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:02.664
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:03:02.664 --> 00:03:04.255
And he said, well, I have an idea.

00:03:04.255 --> 00:03:05.558
Would you like to hear it?

00:03:05.558 --> 00:03:06.391
And I said, yeah.

00:03:06.391 --> 00:03:07.474
So he did come to Nashville.

00:03:07.474 --> 00:03:11.588
We talked it over and the rest
is gonna be history, I hope.

00:03:11.588 --> 00:03:12.421
- I love that.

00:03:12.421 --> 00:03:13.785
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:03:13.785 --> 00:03:16.601
Can you, is it kind of
about the Nashville dream?

00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:18.771
Like, what is the book about?

00:03:18.771 --> 00:03:20.527
- Well, it is about the Nashville dream

00:03:20.527 --> 00:03:22.729
and you know, both of us
Dolly and I, Dolly came

00:03:22.729 --> 00:03:24.262
from the Hills of Tennessee and I came

00:03:24.262 --> 00:03:27.445
from this little town up in
New York state, way, way up.

00:03:27.445 --> 00:03:30.418
And both of us, probably a
million to one chance, you know,

00:03:30.418 --> 00:03:31.681
odds against us making it.

00:03:31.681 --> 00:03:34.613
And the same thing in
this book, Run Rose Run.

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:37.613
The singer, AnnieLee keys, you know,

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:41.260
she's got a lot of talent
and she comes to Nashville,

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.628
but you know, well, you know, Kelly,

00:03:43.628 --> 00:03:44.968
I mean, it's hard to make it.

00:03:44.968 --> 00:03:45.801
- Yeah.

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:46.635
- With all the talent in the world.

00:03:46.635 --> 00:03:48.737
And that's what the story's about.

00:03:48.737 --> 00:03:52.478
She has, her background
is kind of weird, Dolly.

00:03:52.478 --> 00:03:54.928
- Yeah, and my background
is kind of weird.

00:03:54.928 --> 00:03:55.831
(James chuckles)

00:03:55.831 --> 00:03:57.470
No actually, it's about a young girl

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.579
that's trying, like she's running

00:03:58.579 --> 00:04:00.589
from something and running to something.

00:04:00.589 --> 00:04:02.313
You have to read the book to know all,

00:04:02.313 --> 00:04:04.143
get to know all of the things about it.

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:05.732
But it's about her journey.

00:04:05.732 --> 00:04:07.849
And then there's an older character

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:09.084
that was once a big star,

00:04:09.084 --> 00:04:12.802
Ruthanna writer that
she still writes songs,

00:04:12.802 --> 00:04:14.719
she has her own studio, has her own band,

00:04:14.719 --> 00:04:17.552
but she considers herself retired.

00:04:18.429 --> 00:04:22.257
And so Ruthanna kind of
becomes a mentor, you know,

00:04:22.257 --> 00:04:24.130
to AnnieLee and they become friends.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.883
And it's a wonderful
story about bad management

00:04:26.883 --> 00:04:29.238
and about all the things that can happen

00:04:29.238 --> 00:04:30.435
to you in the business.

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:34.845
In addition to AnnieLee
running from this mystery thing

00:04:34.845 --> 00:04:36.423
that is part of the whole book.

00:04:36.423 --> 00:04:37.256
- Yeah.

00:04:37.256 --> 00:04:38.707
- And it's really a clever little book.

00:04:38.707 --> 00:04:41.313
And I was so honored to
get to write some with him.

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:42.689
I was a little intimidated

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:43.522
'cause I've never written a novel.

00:04:43.522 --> 00:04:45.385
- And I relate to Ethan in the book.

00:04:45.385 --> 00:04:46.565
- And you were what James?

00:04:46.565 --> 00:04:47.864
- I relate to Ethan in the book.

00:04:47.864 --> 00:04:51.839
He's a handsome, young, up and
coming singer and, you know,

00:04:51.839 --> 00:04:53.194
but she says, I can't relate to him.

00:04:53.194 --> 00:04:54.741
I gotta relate to the older guys.

00:04:54.741 --> 00:04:55.886
So, all right I'll relate anyway.

00:04:55.886 --> 00:04:57.285
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:04:57.285 --> 00:04:58.118
- No, wait-

00:04:58.118 --> 00:05:00.588
- Yeah, there's Ruthanna's,

00:05:00.588 --> 00:05:03.481
anyway, Ruthanna, she has a manager

00:05:03.481 --> 00:05:05.600
that she loves called
Jack the older woman.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.548
So he's gonna be, he's
saying he can play that

00:05:07.548 --> 00:05:10.238
'cause we are gonna be
doing a movie at some point.

00:05:10.238 --> 00:05:11.400
- I know I heard about that.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.426
(audience clap and cheer)

00:05:12.426 --> 00:05:13.880
I heard that you were gonna make it

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.934
into a movie and I think
that's such a brilliant idea.

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:19.298
I wonder though, Dolly,
any pieces of your life,

00:05:19.298 --> 00:05:20.892
I mean, since James asked you to do this,

00:05:20.892 --> 00:05:22.230
any pieces of your kind of seep

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:26.152
into this character or any of
the characters in the book?

00:05:26.152 --> 00:05:26.985
- Oh, of course.

00:05:26.985 --> 00:05:29.564
I related to every single
one of them, the young girl

00:05:29.564 --> 00:05:32.807
and even Ethan that
James was talking about.

00:05:32.807 --> 00:05:35.421
'Cause I related to all
the young men that I met

00:05:35.421 --> 00:05:38.073
when they were coming to
Nashville, all the people,

00:05:38.073 --> 00:05:41.699
even like, you know, Merle
Haggard, who I loved and he was,

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:44.869
we were getting to make it
pretty much at the same time.

00:05:44.869 --> 00:05:49.257
And then I sang a duet
called Demons from the book,

00:05:49.257 --> 00:05:50.184
one of the songs I wrote

00:05:50.184 --> 00:05:53.971
from the album with
Merle Haggard's son, Ben.

00:05:53.971 --> 00:05:56.249
So I'm really excited about that.

00:05:56.249 --> 00:05:58.120
So there's a lot of things I relate to.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.647
Of course, James has this wonderful mind

00:06:00.647 --> 00:06:02.655
for creating everything,

00:06:02.655 --> 00:06:05.177
but it is something at
least I can relate to,

00:06:05.177 --> 00:06:06.760
and love the story.

00:06:07.623 --> 00:06:09.478
- Yeah. And Kelly, one
of the really cool things

00:06:09.478 --> 00:06:12.211
that what makes this so
different is that Dolly,

00:06:12.211 --> 00:06:14.128
you know, did an album.

00:06:15.101 --> 00:06:16.073
- Yeah, I was about to say that, yeah.

00:06:16.073 --> 00:06:17.546
- The songs that you read about

00:06:17.546 --> 00:06:19.051
in the book and it's amazing.

00:06:19.051 --> 00:06:20.924
And the album is spectacular.

00:06:20.924 --> 00:06:23.635
It's a wonderful, wonderful
album with 12 new songs and-

00:06:23.635 --> 00:06:24.526
- Well, she's Dolly Parton.

00:06:24.526 --> 00:06:26.704
- There's the album, Run Rose Run.

00:06:26.704 --> 00:06:28.084
(Kelly chuckles)

00:06:28.084 --> 00:06:28.917
- But that's her journey.

00:06:28.917 --> 00:06:30.690
(audience clapping) You should
be able to read the book

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.003
and then you listen to
the songs or vice versa,

00:06:34.003 --> 00:06:36.043
start with the songs
and then read the book.

00:06:36.043 --> 00:06:39.754
It's a very, and I don't think
any anybody's ever done it.

00:06:39.754 --> 00:06:41.713
- I was gonna say, it's
a very clever thing too

00:06:41.713 --> 00:06:44.442
because you're not only
into the novel, but you're

00:06:44.442 --> 00:06:47.126
into the music, which creates
a bigger story in your head.

00:06:47.126 --> 00:06:49.815
You know, I always say our
mind doesn't have a budget

00:06:49.815 --> 00:06:51.695
so the best movies are like
in your head almost, you know.

00:06:51.695 --> 00:06:52.703
So you're like creating this

00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:54.758
and you've given us a compilation album,

00:06:54.758 --> 00:06:55.677
like for the album too,

00:06:55.677 --> 00:06:58.651
it's a companion to the book.

00:06:58.651 --> 00:06:59.996
So I'm excited about the movie.

00:06:59.996 --> 00:07:01.141
I'm excited about all of it.

00:07:01.141 --> 00:07:01.974
It's so cool.

00:07:01.974 --> 00:07:03.667
I love like two people that we all love,

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:05.991
working together to create
something that's so magical.

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:06.824
(audience clapping)

00:07:06.824 --> 00:07:08.146
So thank you for doing that.

00:07:08.146 --> 00:07:10.136
Everybody, James and Dolly's book

00:07:10.136 --> 00:07:13.307
and Dolly's new album are
both called Run Rose Run

00:07:13.307 --> 00:07:14.146
and they air out.

00:07:14.146 --> 00:07:15.201
Now you gotta check it out.

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:16.676
Thank y'all so much for calling

00:07:16.676 --> 00:07:18.090
and I appreciate you taking your time.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:18.923
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:07:18.923 --> 00:07:19.756
- All right.

00:07:19.756 --> 00:07:21.198
- Thank you, love you.

